  {
    topic: "CRISPR Gene Editing and India Biotechnology Advances",
    text: "Gene editing refers to technology that can change DNA sequences at one or more points in a strand, including removing or changing a single base or inserting a new gene. India produced its first gene-edited sheep using CRISPR-Cas9 technology, with the Myostatin gene edited to boost muscle growth by 30 percent without adding foreign DNA, making it different from transgenic animals. India also launched BIRSA-101, its first indigenous CRISPR-based gene editing therapy for Sickle Cell Disease, developed by CSIR-IGIB. Separately, ICAR developed India first genome-edited rice varieties, DRR Rice 100 Kamla and Pusa DST Rice, using CRISPR-Cas technology. These varieties contain no foreign DNA and are therefore not classified as GM crops. The key distinction in gene editing lies between somatic genome editing, which cannot be inherited, and germline genome editing, which can be passed to future generations.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["CRISPR-Cas9 mechanism and India's gene editing milestones", "CRISPR-Cas9 uses a small piece of RNA to guide the Cas9 cutting protein to the exact spot in DNA. Three main techniques: ZFNs (Zinc Finger Nucleases), TALENs (Transcription Activator-Like Effector Nucleases), and CRISPR-Cas9. India milestones: (1) First gene-edited sheep - Kashmir University with ICAR, Myostatin gene edited via CRISPR-Cas9, 30% more muscle growth, no foreign DNA added. (2) BIRSA-101 - India's first indigenous CRISPR therapy for Sickle Cell Disease, developed by CSIR-IGIB, dedicated to Bhagwan Birsa Munda on his 150th birth anniversary. (3) DRR Rice 100 Kamla by ICAR-IIRR Hyderabad - improved Samba Mahsuri by targeting CKX2 gene. (4) Pusa DST Rice 1 by ICAR-IARI - improved version of MTU1010 rice. UPSC: Gene editing vs GM crops distinction is a key exam point."],
      ["GM Crops vs Genome-Edited Crops - the regulatory and scientific distinction", "GM (Genetically Modified) Crops have DNA artificially altered, often inserting DNA from another species (transgenics). Example: Bt Cotton contains a gene from Bacillus thuringiensis bacterium. Genome-edited crops use CRISPR to alter existing genes without adding foreign DNA. Regulatory difference in India: Bt Cotton is the only GM crop approved for commercial cultivation. Bt-Brinjal was cleared by GEAC but placed on moratorium. Herbicide-tolerant GM mustard DMH-11 approved but under Supreme Court review. FSSAI under Food Safety and Standards Act 2006 prohibits import/manufacture/sale of GM food without approval. Genome-edited crops with no foreign DNA face a different, lighter regulatory pathway. UPSC GS3: GM crops, GEAC, regulatory framework, biosafety."],
      ["CAR-T Cell Therapy and India's indigenisation of cancer treatment", "CAR-T (Chimeric Antigen Receptor T-cell) Therapy: Patient's T cells collected from blood, genetically modified in laboratory, a special receptor CAR added enabling them to recognise and attack cancer cells. Called a 'living drug' because modified cells continue working inside the body. India's NexCAR19 became first indigenously developed CAR-T therapy, approved by CDSCO for treating blood cancer, developed at IIT Bombay and Tata Memorial Hospital. Cost advantage: Indian CAR-T costs approximately Rs 40 lakh vs Rs 4 crore for imported versions. Advantages: Long-term remission, personalised treatment using patient's own cells, short treatment duration (~45 days). Challenges: Cytokine Release Syndrome (systemic inflammatory response), Antigen Escape (tumours losing targeted antigen), Neurotoxicity. UPSC: indigenisation of medical technology, affordable healthcare."],
      ["GenomeIndia Project and National Biobank", "GenomeIndia Project: Initiated by Department of Biotechnology (DBT) with 20 institutions. Objective: Build comprehensive catalogue of genetic variations reflecting India's unique diversity. Data stored at Indian Biological Data Centre (IBDC) at Faridabad, Haryana - India's first national life science data repository. Preliminary findings published in Nature Genetics journal. Significance: India has 4,600+ population groups with distinct genetic profiles, making it a unique genetic diversity resource. National Biobank under Phenome India (PI-ChECK - Phenome India CSIR Health Cohort Knowledgebase): Launched by CSIR, will collect genomic, lifestyle and clinical data from 10,000 individuals, will power AI-driven diagnostics and gene-guided therapies. Based on UK Biobank model but customised for Indian diversity. UPSC: India's biotechnology infrastructure, precision medicine."],
      ["Mitochondrial Replacement Therapy and Three-Parent Baby", "Mitochondrial diseases are caused by defects in mitochondria, the tiny power plants inside cells that generate over 90% of cellular energy. MRT (Mitochondrial Replacement Therapy) allows babies at high risk of inheriting mitochondrial diseases to be born healthy. Mechanism: The embryo contains nuclear DNA from both parents (carrying all personal traits) plus mitochondria from a healthy egg donor. The child born has three genetic contributors, hence called a Three-Parent Baby. Techniques: Maternal Spindle Transfer (MST) done before fertilisation; Pronuclear Transfer (PNT) done after fertilisation. Clinical trial in UK successfully protected eight babies. India does not currently permit MRT. UPSC: Bioethics questions often test MRT, three-parent baby, and India's regulatory stance on emerging reproductive technologies."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "India's genome-edited rice varieties DRR Rice 100 Kamla and Pusa DST Rice 1 are NOT classified as GM crops because:", opts: ["They were developed by a government institution rather than a private company, so different regulations apply","They contain no foreign DNA from another species - CRISPR was used to modify existing genes within the rice genome itself, which places them outside the GM crop regulatory framework","They have been grown only in laboratory conditions and not released for commercial cultivation, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","The Supreme Court has not yet reviewed their approval, so their classification remains pending"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "BIRSA-101, India's first indigenous CRISPR-based gene therapy for Sickle Cell Disease, was developed by which institution and why is Sickle Cell Disease a priority for India?", opts: ["Developed by IIT Bombay; Sickle Cell Disease is a priority because it affects urban populations disproportionately","Developed by CSIR-IGIB; Sickle Cell Disease is caused by a single gene mutation (HBB gene) making it amenable to gene editing, and it disproportionately affects Scheduled Tribe communities in central India","Developed by AIIMS Delhi; Sickle Cell Disease was selected because it is the most common genetic disorder globally with no existing treatments, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","Developed by ICAR-IARI; Sickle Cell Disease affects agricultural communities dependent on wheat-based diets"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The distinction between somatic genome editing and germline genome editing is significant for bioethics regulation because:", opts: ["Somatic editing is more expensive and technically complex, requiring more regulatory oversight for cost-effectiveness, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply","Germline editing creates genetic changes that can be inherited by future generations who cannot consent, raising profound ethical questions about permanently altering the human species - whereas somatic editing affects only the treated individual","Germline editing only works in adults while somatic editing works in children, creating different safety profiles, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity","Somatic editing is reversible while germline editing is permanent, but both face restrictions in India under the Biological Diversity Act, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's NexCAR19 CAR-T therapy represents indigenisation of advanced cancer treatment. Which aspect makes it significant for India's public health policy beyond the scientific achievement?", opts: ["It uses a different mechanism than imported CAR-T therapies, making it more effective for Indian patients' genetic profiles, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning","It costs approximately Rs 40 lakh compared to Rs 4 crore for imported CAR-T therapies - indigenisation dramatically reduced cost, making a life-saving treatment potentially accessible to middle-income patients rather than only the wealthy","It received WHO approval before Indian approval, demonstrating India's rising global stature in biotechnology, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful","NexCAR19 was developed by a private pharmaceutical company under PLI scheme, demonstrating that production-linked incentives work for advanced therapeutics, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The GenomeIndia Project is compared to the UK Biobank but with a crucial difference in its rationale for India. What makes India's genetic diversity dataset uniquely valuable?", opts: ["India's large population size means statistical findings will be more significant than those from smaller countries, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","India has approximately 4,600 distinct population groups with separate ancestral histories due to centuries of endogamy, creating a genetic diversity that a single reference genome cannot capture and that has direct implications for disease risk prediction and drug response in Indian patients","India's warm climate creates unique environmental pressures on the genome that differ from European populations, requiring separate baseline studies, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain resilience,","Indian diets high in spices create epigenetic profiles not seen in Western biobanks, making India data necessary for global nutrition science, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful late-developing economies"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "India Space Programme: NISAR, Gaganyaan, SpaDeX and NavIC",
    text: "India's space programme achieved multiple milestones in 2025. NISAR, the NASA-ISRO Synthetic Aperture Radar satellite, was launched in July 2025 using ISRO's GSLV-F16 into a Sun Synchronous Polar Orbit. It is the world's first Earth observation satellite integrating both L-band and S-band SAR on a single platform. The SpaDeX mission demonstrated India's indigenous docking technology called the Bhartiya Docking System, achieving successful satellite docking in low Earth orbit. The Axiom-4 mission carried Indian astronaut Group Captain Shubhanshu Shukla to the International Space Station, marking India's return to human spaceflight. During Operation Sindoor, India used NavIC across combat operations including missile guidance and drone navigation. NavIC consists of 7 satellites providing accurate position, velocity and timing service up to 1,500 km beyond India's landmass.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["NISAR Mission - technical details and significance", "NISAR (NASA-ISRO Synthetic Aperture Radar): World's first EO satellite with both L-band (NASA) and S-band (ISRO) SAR on single platform. First GSLV rocket launch into Sun-Synchronous Orbit (SSO) - GSLV normally used for Geosynchronous Transfer Orbit. Applications: SweepSAR produces high-resolution, wide-swath images of terrain. Monitors: glacier movement, earthquake deformation, forest biomass, wetlands, sea ice, groundwater changes. Mission life 5 years. Significance for UPSC: India-USA space cooperation; dual-use of SAR for both civilian monitoring and strategic surveillance capability. Every monsoon, NISAR-type data informs flood mapping. The L-band penetrates clouds and vegetation - critical for India's monsoon-season observations."],
      ["SpaDeX Mission and India's docking technology", "SpaDeX (Space Docking Experiment): India became only the 4th country to demonstrate space docking technology after USA, Russia, and China. Spacecraft: SDX01 (Chaser) and SDX02 (Target) in low Earth circular orbit. Docking technology called Bhartiya Docking System (BDS). Strategic significance: Docking is essential for (1) Bharatiya Antariksh Station (India's planned space station by 2035); (2) Moon mission requiring multiple launches assembled in orbit; (3) Satellite servicing and refuelling; (4) Human spaceflight missions beyond low Earth orbit. Developed by UR Rao Satellite Centre (URSC), Bengaluru. UPSC: India's space ambition timeline, docking vs rendezvous distinction, Bharatiya Antariksh Station plans."],
      ["Gaganyaan Programme and human spaceflight milestone", "Gaganyaan: India's first Human Space Flight mission. Crew of 3 to 400km Low Earth Orbit for 3-day mission. Launch Vehicle: LVM-3 (formerly GSLV Mk-III) - 3-stage rocket with cryogenic upper stage. First uncrewed mission will carry AI-powered Gynoid (female robot) Vyommitra. Success will make India only 4th country with crewed spacecraft capability after USA, Russia, China. Axiom-4 (Ax-4) Mission context: Group Captain Shubhanshu Shukla flew to ISS aboard SpaceX Dragon in July 2025, returning July 15, 2025 - this was India's precursor human spaceflight experience before Gaganyaan. Programme scope extended to include building Bharatiya Antariksh Station. UPSC: Indian astronaut corps, Vyommitra, LVM-3 capabilities."],
      ["NavIC - India's indigenous navigation system and Operation Sindoor use", "NavIC (Navigation with Indian Constellation): India's own satellite navigation system developed by ISRO. Earlier called IRNSS (Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System). Constellation: 7 satellites - 3 in geostationary orbit, 4 in inclined geosynchronous orbit. Coverage: Accurate PVT (Position, Velocity, Timing) up to 1,500 km beyond Indian landmass. Dual-band: L5 and S-band signals. Operation Sindoor use (2025): Missile guidance, drone navigation, Battle Damage Assessment - demonstrating NavIC as a mature defence navigation system. Significance: Strategic autonomy in navigation - GPS is US-controlled and can be denied. India's NavIC ensures India cannot be deprived of navigation capability during conflict. UPSC GS3: Space technology, indigenisation, dual-use technology."],
      ["Cryogenic Engine and India's launch vehicle capability", "Cryogenic engines use both cryogenic fuel (liquid hydrogen, liquefied at -253°C) and cryogenic oxidizer (liquid oxygen, at -183°C). Used in the upper stage of rockets because cryogenic propellants provide highest specific impulse (fuel efficiency) of any chemical propellant combination. CE20: India's largest cryogenic engine, developed by Liquid Propulsion Systems Centre, Valiamala, Kerala. Powers LVM-3 upper stage. Qualified for Gaganyaan human missions. Semi-cryogenic engines: Combine cryogenic oxidizer (LOX) with conventional hydrocarbon fuel (kerosene) - India's SCE-200 under development. Mastery of cryogenic technology (achieved 2014, GSLV D5) ended dependence on Russian cryogenic engines. UPSC: Technology indigenisation, strategic self-reliance in space, ISRO centres and their specialisations."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "NISAR satellite was launched using GSLV-F16 into Sun-Synchronous Orbit (SSO). Why is this launch significant beyond the satellite's Earth observation mission?", opts: ["It was India's first satellite launched in partnership with NASA, marking the beginning of India-USA strategic cooperation in space","It was the first time a GSLV rocket placed a satellite in Sun-Synchronous Orbit - GSLV is normally used for Geosynchronous Transfer Orbit, demonstrating expanded launch vehicle versatility","NISAR's L-band SAR was the first Indian radar satellite, making India independent of foreign satellite imagery for the first time, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","The launch proved India's ability to build heavy payloads exceeding 5 tonnes, qualifying ISRO for commercial heavy-lift contracts"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's SpaDeX mission made India the 4th country to demonstrate space docking technology. Which of the following BEST explains why docking capability is described as a prerequisite for India's Bharatiya Antariksh Station?", opts: ["Space stations require regular crew rotation using Soyuz-type capsules which can only dock, not land on the station, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with cross-cutting reforms in factor","A permanent space station cannot be launched as a single rocket payload due to weight and size constraints - it must be assembled in orbit from multiple launches using docking, making docking a foundational enabling technology for any space station programme","Docking technology is required for GPS satellite maintenance which is the commercial justification for the Bharatiya Antariksh Station, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise","International Space Station protocols require all visiting spacecraft to have domestic docking systems as a safety redundancy requirement, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning curve"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Group Captain Shubhanshu Shukla's participation in the Axiom-4 mission to the International Space Station is described as relevant to Gaganyaan. What is the connection?", opts: ["The ISS visit was a diplomatic courtesy extended by NASA following India-US 2+2 dialogue, with no technical relevance to Gaganyaan, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that","Axiom-4 gave an Indian astronaut-designate direct experience of spaceflight, microgravity adaptation, ISS systems, and international space operations - creating institutional knowledge within India's human spaceflight programme before Gaganyaan launches India's own crew","Shubhanshu Shukla will command the first Gaganyaan mission, and the Axiom-4 mission was mandatory certification required by ISRO's crewed mission safety protocols, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","NASA required an Indian astronaut on the ISS as a condition for approving NISAR's L-band SAR export licence to India, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from geographic concentration"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "NavIC's use during Operation Sindoor for missile guidance and drone navigation demonstrates which strategic principle about indigenous technology?", opts: ["Indian weapons systems are now superior to imported alternatives, allowing India to discontinue all defence imports immediately, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity","Strategic navigation infrastructure controlled by a foreign power (GPS by USA) can in theory be degraded or denied during conflict - NavIC ensures India retains navigation sovereignty in its own region regardless of geopolitical tensions with GPS-owning nations","NavIC's use in Sindoor proved that India's satellites are hardened against electronic warfare, unlike American GPS satellites, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with cross-cutting reforms in","The Sindoor operation demonstrated that satellite navigation technology can substitute for human intelligence, reducing casualty risk, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation,"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The distinction between cryogenic and semi-cryogenic rocket engines is relevant to India's future launch vehicle capability because:", opts: ["Cryogenic engines face restrictions by the Missile Technology Control Regime (MTCR) for use in ballistic missiles, while semi-cryogenic engines are permitted - India needs semi-cryogenic engines to remain MTCR compliant, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful","Semi-cryogenic engines (LOX + kerosene) can be stored ready-to-launch unlike cryogenic engines which must be fuelled immediately before launch - this storability advantage makes semi-cryogenic engines suitable for rapid-response military launch vehicles, whereas cryogenic engines are better suited for planned civilian missions requiring maximum efficiency","Cryogenic engines cannot be reused while semi-cryogenic engines can be recovered and refurbished, making semi-cryogenic technology essential for India's reusable launch vehicle programme, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Semi-cryogenic engines produce thrust five times greater than cryogenic engines of the same size, making them necessary for India's heavy-lift ambitions, which reflects the Survey's broader argument about balancing short-term efficiency costs against long-term strategic resilience in sectors where supply chain concentration creates asymmetric vulnerability"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "Maratha Military Landscapes, Chola Dynasty and UNESCO Heritage",
    text: "Maratha Military Landscapes of India, comprising 12 forts across Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List as India's 44th heritage site. These forts evolved during the 17th century reign of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj through the Peshwa period in the 19th century. The forts are situated across the Sahyadri mountain ranges, Konkan Coast, Deccan Plateau and Eastern Ghats. India now holds 44 World Heritage Sites, ranked 6th globally and 2nd in the Asia-Pacific region, with 36 cultural, 7 natural and 1 mixed site. Separately, UNESCO added manuscripts of the Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra to its Memory of the World Register, bringing India's total entries in the International Register to 13. The Natyashastra, attributed to sage Bharata Muni, is the foundational text of Indian classical performing arts.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["Maratha Empire - military architecture and administrative system", "Maratha Military Landscapes: 12 forts - 11 in Maharashtra (including Shivneri birthplace of Shivaji, Rajgad first capital, Raigad second capital) and 1 in Tamil Nadu. Chhatrapati Shivaji's administrative system: Ashtapradhan (council of 8 ministers) including Peshwa (PM), Amatya (Finance), Senapati (Commander), Sumant (Foreign), Nyayadhyaksh (Chief Justice), Panditrao (High Priest). Revenue: Sardeshmukhi (10% tax on Maratha territories), Chauth (25% levy on neighbouring territories). Decline: Third Battle of Panipat (1761) against Ahmad Shah Abdali and three Anglo-Maratha Wars. UPSC: Maratha administration, revenue system, and decline are standard Medieval India questions. The UNESCO designation connects medieval history to current affairs."],
      ["UNESCO World Heritage Sites - India's status and categories", "India's WHS count: 44 total (36 Cultural, 7 Natural, 1 Mixed) - ranked 6th globally and 2nd in Asia-Pacific. World Heritage Convention: 1972 UNESCO Convention - sites selected for Outstanding Universal Value (OUV). Three categories: Cultural (architecture, historic sites, living traditions), Natural (outstanding ecology, biodiversity, geology), Mixed (both). World Heritage Centre based in Paris, France, established 1992. Maratha Military Landscapes selection criteria: demonstrates architectural and military ingenuity combining hill forts, coastal forts, and plateau forts across diverse terrain over 17th-19th centuries. Tentative List: India has submitted multiple sites including Meghalayan Age Caves, Natural Heritage of Tirumala Hills, Varkala Coast, Deccan Traps at Panchgani, St Mary's Island Cluster, and Salkhan Fossil Park for future consideration."],
      ["UNESCO Memory of the World Register and Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra inscription", "UNESCO Memory of the World (MoW) Programme: Launched 1992. Purpose: Preserve documentary heritage, facilitate access, enhance awareness - particularly for areas affected by conflict or natural disaster. Current status: 570 inscriptions in International Register since 1997. India's 13 entries include: Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra manuscripts (2025), Abhinavagupta manuscripts, Gilgit Manuscripts, First NAM Summit Archives. India's nodal agency: Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts (IGNCA) since 2014. About Natyashastra: Attributed to sage Bharata Muni, describes principles of performing arts (dance, drama, music). Contains Natyotpatti (origin story of performing art) and is the first text classifying Indian musical instruments. About Bhagavad Gita: 700-verse dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna in Mahabharata's Bhishma Parva. UPSC: Memory of World vs World Heritage vs Intangible Heritage - three separate UNESCO programmes often confused."],
      ["Chola Dynasty and its temple architecture and naval achievements", "Imperial Chola Dynasty (9th-13th century CE): Rose under Vijayalaya Chola, reached zenith under Rajaraja I (985 CE) and Rajendra I (1014-1044 CE). Key achievements: Rajendra I conquered Ganga Valley (title Gangaikonda Cholan), led naval expeditions to Sri Lanka and Srivijaya Empire in Southeast Asia - one of ancient India's greatest naval campaigns. Administrative evidence: Uttaramerur inscriptions detail Chola elections and village governance. Three UNESCO WHS temples: Brihadeeswara Temple, Thanjavur (Rajaraja I, 1010 CE); Gangaikonda Cholapuram (Rajendra I, 1035 CE); Airavatesvara Temple, Darasuram (Rajaraja II, 12th century). All three are Dravidian architecture. Chola Gangam Lake: Reservoir built by Rajendra Chola I, connected to Kollidam River (Kaveri distributary), still functions as water source. UPSC: Chola history, naval power, temple architecture, UNESCO sites."],
      ["Lucknow as UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy", "Lucknow became India's second UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy after Hyderabad. UNESCO Creative Cities Network (UCCN): Recognises cities using culture and creativity as drivers of sustainable urban development across 7 creative fields: Crafts and Folk Art, Design, Film, Gastronomy, Literature, Media Arts, Music. Lucknow's Awadhi cuisine: Nawab Asaf-ud-daulah (1775) made Lucknow the Awadh capital; the Dum Pukht slow-cooking technique developed under royal patronage for kebabs, kormas, biryanis, sheermal. Other Lucknow features: Bara Imambara, Chota Imambara, Rumi Darwaza (architecture); Chikankari embroidery (GI tagged craft); Kathak dance tradition; Lakhauri bricks and fish motif Nawabi architectural features. Historical nicknames: 'Constantinople of the East' for arts and culture; 'Golden City of India' for refinement. UPSC: UCCN categories, India's creative cities, Awadhi culture and architecture."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "Maratha Military Landscapes becoming India's 44th UNESCO World Heritage Site is specifically significant in the context of UPSC preparation because:", opts: ["It suggests that Indian history is more important than Indian geography for UPSC preparation, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain resilience, making","It connects three distinct areas of GS1: Medieval India (Maratha Empire), Geography (Sahyadri, Deccan Plateau, Konkan), and Current Affairs (UNESCO inscription) - demonstrating how UPSC creates integrated questions spanning multiple syllabus areas","The UNESCO inscription provides legal protection to these forts under Indian law for the first time, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather","It indicates UPSC will set questions only on Maharashtra-related history in upcoming examinations, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that commercial"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Ashtapradhan system of the Maratha Empire is often compared to modern cabinet governance. Which feature of the Ashtapradhan most closely resembles modern democratic governance?", opts: ["The Peshwa (Prime Minister) position, because Peshwas eventually became de facto rulers - demonstrating how executive power can be delegated and centralised","The inclusion of Nyayadhyaksh (Chief Justice) and Panditrao (High Priest) as members demonstrates separation of judicial and religious functions from executive power - conceptually similar to modern separation of powers","The Ashtapradhan had fixed terms for each minister, creating a rotation principle similar to modern democratic accountability","The system required unanimous consent of all 8 ministers before any policy decision, functioning like modern cabinet collective responsibility"], ans: 0 },
      { q: "UNESCO's Memory of the World Programme, World Heritage Sites Programme, and Intangible Cultural Heritage Programme are three separate UNESCO mechanisms. The Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra were inscribed in Memory of the World specifically because:", opts: ["They are the most important spiritual texts in the world and UNESCO ranked them above all other world scriptures, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from","Memory of the World protects DOCUMENTARY HERITAGE (manuscripts, records, archives) - the Gita and Natyashastra were inscribed as historical manuscript collections, not as living traditions or built structures which fall under the other two programmes","UNESCO's Memory of World programme covers all of India's traditional knowledge systems under a single designation, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain","The inscription gives India copyright protection over the Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra texts under international intellectual property law, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Rajendra Chola I's naval expedition against the Srivijaya Empire in Southeast Asia is described as one of India's greatest ancient naval achievements. Which current affairs connection makes this historically relevant?", opts: ["The modern Indonesian and Malaysian navies trace their lineage directly to the Srivijaya navy defeated by Rajendra Chola","The name Sri Vijaya Puram was given to the Andaman and Nicobar Islands capital in recognition of this naval victory, connecting ancient Chola naval power to modern India's strategic interest in the Indo-Pacific and the Andaman Sea","ASEAN members consider the Chola naval expedition a precedent for India's claim to exclusive economic zones in the Bay of Bengal, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","The Chola naval routes established the original maritime Silk Road, making Rajendra Chola the founder of India's export economy"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Lucknow's UNESCO Creative City of Gastronomy designation reflects which broader principle about intangible culture?", opts: ["UNESCO values gastronomy only in cities with a royal or imperial history, explaining why Lucknow and Hyderabad, both former capitals, qualified, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with cross-cutting reforms in factor markets, logistics","Culinary traditions are a vehicle for transmitting social practices, agricultural knowledge, community identity and historical memory across generations - Awadhi Dum Pukht cuisine embodies Nawabi court culture, Mughal-Awadhi architectural fusion, and localised agricultural products in a living tradition","The designation is primarily an economic classification to promote culinary tourism rather than a recognition of cultural heritage, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","UNESCO's Creative Cities of Gastronomy designation reflects a city's demonstrated ability to leverage its culinary heritage for sustainable urban development through tourism, food entrepreneurship and cultural events, and Lucknow's designation reflects the UP government's active investment in its Awadhi cuisine brand alongside heritage preservation programmes"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "Social Reformers: Arya Samaj, Dadabhai Naoroji, Sree Narayana Guru",
    text: "The year 2025 marked multiple reform movement anniversaries. Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati in Bombay in 1875, celebrated its 150th anniversary. Dadabhai Naoroji's 200th birth anniversary was observed nationally. Naoroji was known as the Grand Old Man of India and served three times as president of the Indian National Congress in 1886, 1893 and 1906. His Drain of Wealth theory demonstrated how British rule systematically transferred India's economic resources to Britain. The Mahasamadhi centenary of Sree Narayana Guru was inaugurated by India's President. Sree Narayana Guru was a social reformer from Kerala who emphasised that real liberation comes through knowledge and compassion, teaching the principle of one caste, one religion, one God for all human beings.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["Arya Samaj - founding, contributions and role in national movement", "Arya Samaj: Founded by Maharshi Dayanand Saraswati, 1875, Bombay. A branch at Lahore established 1877. Nature: Revivalist movement for religious and social reform of Hindu society through return to Vedic religion. Key contributions: (1) Religious: Promoted monotheistic Hindu order, pioneered Shuddhi (purification/reconversion ceremony). (2) Social: Opposed untouchability and caste discrimination. (3) Education: DAV (Dayanand Anglo-Vedic) schools (first established Lahore 1886 by Lala Lajpat Rai); Gurukul Kangri (1902, Haridwar, Swami Shraddhananda). (4) National Movement: Supported Hindi language, Khadi, Swadeshi; opposed salt taxes. Leaders inspired: Lala Lajpat Rai, Bhagat Singh, Ram Prasad Bismil. Hyderabad Satyagraha (1938-39) against Nizam's prohibition on Vedic teachings. UPSC: 19th century social reform movements; Dayanand Saraswati biography."],
      ["Dadabhai Naoroji - Drain of Wealth Theory and political contributions", "Dadabhai Naoroji (1825-1917): Called Grand Old Man of India and Unofficial Ambassador of India. Three-time INC President (1886, 1893, 1906 Calcutta Session - where he bridged moderates and extremists). First Indian Full-Professor at Elphinstone College. Professor of Gujarati at University College London. Founded Rast Goftar newspaper; co-founded Rahnumai Mazdayasan Sabha (1851) to reform Parsi society. Economic contribution: Drain of Wealth Theory - demonstrated through statistical analysis that British India's revenue surplus was transferred to Britain as Home Charges (salaries, pensions, interest on debt) rather than reinvested in India, impoverishing the country. Published 'Poverty and Un-British Rule in India' (1901). Social reform: Founded Student Literary and Scientific Society (1848), established 6 girls' schools by 1849. UPSC: Economic nationalism, Moderate phase of nationalism, Parsi community contributions."],
      ["Sree Narayana Guru - social philosophy and anti-caste movement in Kerala", "Sree Narayana Guru (1856-1928): Born in Chempazhanthy near Thiruvananthapuram to Ezhava family (backward untouchable community). Key philosophy: 'One Caste, One Religion, One God for all human beings.' Real liberation through knowledge and compassion, not blind faith. Education as supreme panacea for social evils - advocated equal opportunity for women. Founded Advaita Ashram at Aluva (1913): dedicated to 'Om Sahodaryam Sarvatra' (all men are equal in the eyes of God). His followers call him Gurudevan. Significance: Led temple consecrations violating caste norms; provided philosophical foundation for Ezhava community's social mobility; influenced Kerala's social reform distinctiveness. UPSC: 19th-20th century social reform; Kerala social movements; caste reform personalities."],
      ["Birsa Munda and tribal resistance movements", "Birsa Munda (1875-1900): Born in Ulihatu, Jharkhand, to Munda tribal community. Teachings: Monotheism (founded Birsait sect), revival of tribal faith rejecting Christian missionary influence, emphasis on cleanliness, hard work, avoiding alcohol. Munda Rebellion (Ulgulan - Great Tumult) 1899-1900: Launched against British colonial exploitation and zamindari system. Slogan: 'Abua Raj Setarjana, Maharani Raj Tundujana' (End British rule, establish our kingdom). Causes: Permanent Settlement Act 1793 abolished traditional Khuntkatti (clan-based communal land ownership by forest clearers); breakdown of traditional governance systems; Christian missionary influence perceived as threat to Munda identity. Died in Ranchi Jail due to cholera in 1900. Remembered as Bhagwan (God) and Dharti Aaba (Father of Earth). November 15 is Janjatiya Gaurav Divas (Tribal Pride Day). UPSC: Tribal uprisings, colonial land policies, adivasi leadership."],
      ["Self-Respect Movement, Vaikom Satyagraha and anti-caste movements", "Self-Respect Movement: Founded by E.V. Ramasamy (Periyar, 1879-1973) in 1925 in Tamil Nadu. Centenary in 2025. Core ideas: Social equality, scientific rationalism essential for egalitarian society. Influenced by Jyotirao Phule and B.R. Ambedkar. Initially inspired by Gandhi's constructive programmes. Periyar published Kudi Arasu (Tamil weekly). In 1932 founded Self-Respect Socialist Party. Vaikom Satyagraha (1924-25): First organised agitation for the right of lower castes to use public roads near Vaikom temple in Kerala. Key leaders: T.K. Madhavan, K. Kelappan, George Joseph, Periyar. Gandhi visited March 1925. Women leaders: Nagammai, S.R. Kannamma, Bhagyam Stanumalaya Perumal. Supporters: Sree Narayana Guru, Chatampi Swamikal. Outcome: Roads opened to all. Significance: Vaikom Satyagraha was the model for subsequent temple entry movements. UPSC: Social reform, Periyar, self-respect movement, Vaikom Satyagraha - frequently tested."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "Dadabhai Naoroji's Drain of Wealth theory was significant not just economically but politically because:", opts: ["It proved that Indians were economically superior to Britons and therefore deserved equal status in the colonial administration, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that commercial cost-benefit","It provided a statistical and economic basis for nationalist demands - demonstrating through British government's own data that colonial rule impoverished India, giving the moderate nationalist movement a factual foundation to argue that British rule was harmful rather than beneficial","The theory was used to negotiate higher Indian representation in the Indian Civil Service examinations, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","It showed that British industrialisation was primarily funded by Indian revenues, leading Britain to offer dominion status to India in 1909, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from geographic"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Arya Samaj's Shuddhi movement created social tensions in British India because:", opts: ["It promoted cow protection leagues that led to communal violence between Hindus and Muslims in urban centres, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than","It claimed to reconvert Muslims and Christians back to Hinduism, which both communities and the British administration perceived as aggressive proselytisation, creating counter-mobilisation - demonstrating how social reform and communal conflict could be linked","It rejected English education in DAV schools, leading to violent conflicts with colonial education authorities, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with cross-cutting reforms in factor markets,","The Shuddhi ceremony required government registration, which the colonial administration used as grounds to declare Arya Samaj an illegal organisation, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Sree Narayana Guru's founding of the Advaita Ashram at Aluva with the motto 'Om Sahodaryam Sarvatra' represents which philosophical approach to caste reform?", opts: ["He used political power through the Kerala Legislative Council to pass anti-discrimination legislation, combining spiritual and legal reform, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful late-developing","He grounded anti-caste activism in Advaita (non-dual) Vedantic philosophy - arguing that since all souls are ultimately one (Advaita), caste distinctions are metaphysically false as well as socially harmful, providing a scriptural argument against caste within the Hindu tradition itself","He rejected all Hindu philosophy as inherently caste-supportive and created a new non-religious secular framework for the Ezhava community, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that commercial","Narayana Guru adopted Christian missionary education methods while rejecting Christian theology, combining Western educational systems with Hindu spiritual traditions, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Birsa Munda's Ulgulan (Great Tumult) uprising of 1899 targeted both British colonial authorities and local landlords. What specific colonial policy most directly caused the Munda community's dispossession?", opts: ["The Rowlatt Act 1919, which allowed detention without trial and was used against tribal leaders including Birsa Munda, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain resilience, making cost-benefit","The Permanent Settlement Act 1793 which abolished the traditional Khuntkatti system - the clan-based communal land ownership by those who cleared forests - by imposing individual land titles that outsiders could purchase, stripping the Munda community of their ancestral land rights","The Forest Act 1878 which declared all forests as state property, criminalising the Munda community's traditional practice of shifting cultivation, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity","The Criminal Tribes Act 1871 which classified Munda people as a criminal tribe, denying them the right to own land or access courts, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that commercial"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Vaikom Satyagraha (1924-25) is described as a model for subsequent temple entry and anti-untouchability movements. What made it a methodological breakthrough in the social reform tradition?", opts: ["It was the first time Gandhi personally led an agitation, giving it national visibility and linking social reform to the mainstream Congress programme, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","It combined constitutional petitioning, non-violent satyagraha, women's participation, cross-community solidarity (Hindus, Muslims, Christians and international supporters), and moral pressure on the King of Travancore - demonstrating that sustained, organised, principled agitation could achieve social reform without violent revolution","The Vaikom Satyagraha was the first agitation where untouchable communities directly occupied temples, establishing a legal precedent for temple entry, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","It was uniquely successful because Periyar's personal friendship with the Maharaja of Travancore provided a back-channel negotiation that resolved the conflict diplomatically"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  }

,
  {
    topic: "Nuclear Energy: Fast Breeder Reactor, Thorium and India Three-Stage Programme",
    text: "India is the only country pursuing a three-stage nuclear programme designed specifically to exploit its vast thorium reserves, the world's largest. The first stage uses Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors burning natural uranium to produce plutonium. The second stage, now entering commissioning with the 500 MWe Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor at Kalpakkam, uses plutonium-uranium mixed oxide fuel and breeds more fissile material than it consumes. The third stage will use uranium-233 produced by irradiating thorium in the second stage. Thorium-232, the only naturally occurring thorium isotope, must be converted to fissile uranium-233 inside a reactor before it can generate power. India is also developing a Thorium Molten Salt Reactor in parallel with BARC's Advanced Heavy Water Reactor as technology demonstrators. China became the first country to achieve thorium-to-uranium fuel conversion in an operational molten salt reactor, which uses liquid salt as both fuel carrier and coolant, operates at atmospheric pressure unlike pressurised water reactors, and has passive safety features where a salt plug melts if temperature rises above safe limits.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["India Three-Stage Nuclear Programme - complete architecture", "Stage 1: Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs) using natural uranium-238 fuel, moderated by heavy water. Produces plutonium-239 as a by-product. Currently operational - India has 22 reactors at 7 stations. Stage 2: Fast Breeder Reactors (FBRs) fuelled by plutonium-uranium MOX (Mixed Oxide Fuel). Breeds more plutonium from uranium-238 blanket AND begins converting thorium-232 into uranium-233. The 500 MWe PFBR at Kalpakkam (Madras Atomic Power Station) is being commissioned by BHAVINI. Uses liquid sodium as coolant (not water) because sodium does not slow down fast neutrons needed for breeding. Stage 3: Advanced Heavy Water Reactors or Thorium MSRs using uranium-233 as primary fuel with thorium-232. Completes the thorium utilisation cycle. Strategic rationale: India has only 2% of global uranium reserves but 25% of thorium reserves - the programme converts an abundant resource into fuel security. UPSC: Three-stage programme structure, BHAVINI, BARC, thorium cycle logic."],
      ["Fast Breeder Reactor vs conventional reactor - key distinctions", "Conventional PHWRs: Use thermal (slow) neutrons; cannot breed significant new fuel; burn uranium-235 (only 0.7% of natural uranium). Fast Breeder Reactors: Use fast (unmoderated) neutrons; breed more fissile material (plutonium-239) from fertile uranium-238 than they consume; conversion ratio above 1.0 makes them net fuel producers. Liquid sodium coolant in FBR: Used because (1) sodium does not moderate (slow) neutrons preserving their fast character; (2) sodium has excellent heat transfer properties; (3) operates at atmospheric pressure reducing mechanical stress. Risks of sodium coolant: Reacts violently with water and air; requires inert atmosphere and double-wall piping. BHAVINI (Bharatiya Nabhikiya Vidyut Nigam Limited): Established by government to construct and operate PFBR. Mahi Banswara Rajasthan Atomic Power Project (MBRAPP): 4 x 700 MWe PHWRs under fleet mode initiative, joint venture of NPCIL and NTPC under ASHVINI. UPSC GS3: nuclear policy, BHAVINI, ASHVINI, fleet mode."],
      ["Thorium Molten Salt Reactor - technical features and India advantage", "Thorium-232 characteristics: Most abundant naturally occurring thorium isotope; three times more abundant than uranium globally; India has world's largest reserves concentrated in monazite sands of coastal Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Odisha. Must be bred into U-233 in a reactor - cannot be used as fuel directly. Molten Salt Reactor (MSR/TMSR): Fourth-generation design; uses high-temperature molten fluoride salt as both coolant and fuel carrier; operates near atmospheric pressure (no pressurised vessel needed); passive safety - freeze plug melts on overheating, draining fuel into a containment tank and stopping reaction automatically. China's TMSR at Wuwei, Gansu: World's only operational MSR as of 2025; achieved first thorium-to-uranium-233 fuel conversion. India's development: Advanced Heavy Water Reactor (AHWR) by BARC as thorium technology demonstrator; Indian Molten Salt Breeder Reactor (IMSBR) concept. Small High-Level Waste Footprint: Thorium produces fewer long-lived minor actinides than plutonium fuels, reducing waste management burden. ITER connection: India's nuclear expertise also contributes to ITER fusion project in France; India manufactured the cryostat housing."],
      ["Nuclear Command Authority and Strategic Forces - governance of nuclear arsenal", "India's nuclear doctrine: No First Use (NFU) pledge - will not use nuclear weapons first; massive retaliation if attacked with nuclear, biological or chemical weapons. Nuclear Command Authority (NCA): Political Council headed by PM makes decisions; Executive Council headed by NSA prepares for execution. Strategic Forces Command (SFC): Responsible for management, deployment and targeting of nuclear weapons under NCA. Agni-5 MIRV capability: Multiple Independently Targetable Reentry Vehicle (MIRV) technology means one missile can deliver multiple warheads to separate targets simultaneously, placing India among USA, Russia, China, France, and UK with MIRV-capable ICBMs. Range: Agni-5 exceeds 5,000 km. Agni Prime (new generation): Two-stage solid-fuel canister missile; rail-based mobile launcher system enhances survivability - rail mobility makes pre-emptive targeting extremely difficult. IGMDP (Integrated Guided Missile Development Programme, 1983): Produced Prithvi (surface-to-surface), Trishul (surface-to-air short range), Akash (medium range SAM), Nag (anti-tank), and Agni series. UPSC: NFU doctrine, NCA, SFC, MIRV, IGMDP milestones."],
      ["ITER Fusion Project and India contribution", "Nuclear fusion vs fission: Fission splits heavy atoms (uranium, plutonium) releasing energy; fusion combines light atoms (hydrogen isotopes deuterium and tritium) releasing far more energy per kilogram of fuel. Fusion advantages: Fuel from seawater (deuterium) and lithium (for tritium breeding); no long-lived radioactive waste; no chain reaction meltdown risk; effectively unlimited fuel supply. ITER (International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor): Located in Saint-Paul-les-Durance, France; objective to demonstrate net energy gain from fusion; funded by EU, USA, Russia, China, India, Japan, South Korea. India's specific contributions: Cryostat (enormous double-wall stainless steel vessel housing entire tokamak, designed and built by ITER-India/IPR Ahmedabad); cryogenic lines carrying liquid helium to cool superconducting magnets; other specialised components. Institute for Plasma Research (IPR), Ahmedabad: India's nodal institution for ITER. India's own tokamaks: ADITYA (first indigenous), ADITYA-U, SST-1 (medium-sized superconducting). Tokamak principle: Plasma (fourth state of matter) confined by magnetic fields in a doughnut-shaped (toroidal) chamber; JET (UK), EAST (China), KSTAR (South Korea), JT-60SA (Japan-Europe) are other major tokamaks. UPSC: Energy security, climate change, India role in international science collaborations."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "India's three-stage nuclear programme is designed to eventually exploit thorium rather than uranium as its primary fuel source. Which statement most precisely explains why Stage 2 (Fast Breeder Reactor) is structurally necessary before Stage 3 (thorium utilisation) can begin at scale?", opts: ["Stage 2 reactors produce the uranium-233 directly from thorium-232 through neutron bombardment, and this U-233 is then used in Stage 3 reactors without any intermediate processing steps, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Fast Breeder Reactors in Stage 2 produce the plutonium from uranium-238 that is needed to start Stage 3 thorium reactors, while simultaneously demonstrating the fast neutron technology required to convert thorium-232 into fissile uranium-233 - thorium cannot initiate a chain reaction on its own and needs fissile material to begin the breeding process","Stage 2 is necessary only to reduce India's uranium import dependency during the transition period before Stage 3 thorium reactors become economically competitive with conventional designs, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","The PFBR in Stage 2 uses thorium as a direct fuel mixed with plutonium in the MOX fuel assemblies, so Stage 2 and Stage 3 effectively run simultaneously rather than sequentially, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Liquid sodium is used as coolant in India's Fast Breeder Reactor at Kalpakkam rather than the heavy water used in Stage 1 PHWRs. This choice reflects which fundamental design requirement of a breeder reactor?", opts: ["Sodium has a higher boiling point than water (883°C vs 100°C) allowing the reactor to operate at higher temperatures and achieve better thermal efficiency in electricity generation compared to water-cooled designs","Fast Breeder Reactors must maintain neutrons at high (fast) energies to achieve a breeding ratio above 1.0 - water and heavy water would moderate (slow) neutrons and destroy the fast neutron spectrum essential for breeding more plutonium from uranium-238 than the reactor consumes","Sodium is used because it is non-reactive with the uranium-plutonium MOX fuel, preventing the fuel rod corrosion that heavy water causes in long-duration reactor operations, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","Liquid sodium enables the reactor to operate at atmospheric pressure, making it safer than pressurised water reactors, and this pressure advantage was the primary reason for its selection over heavy water"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "China's Thorium Molten Salt Reactor at Wuwei achieved a milestone significant for India's long-term energy strategy. Which feature of the molten salt design is most relevant to India's Stage 3 nuclear ambitions?", opts: ["The MSR produces electricity 40% more efficiently than conventional reactors, which would make India's future thorium-based power plants commercially competitive without government subsidies","The MSR demonstrated actual thorium-to-uranium-233 conversion in an operating reactor - since India must convert its thorium-232 reserves into fissile U-233 before Stage 3 can function, China's proof-of-concept validates the technical pathway that India's entire long-term nuclear strategy depends upon","The MSR operates at atmospheric pressure without a pressurised vessel, dramatically reducing construction costs and making it economically feasible to build Stage 3 reactors in remote Indian coastal areas where thorium monazite sands are found, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","Molten salt reactors generate no radioactive waste since the liquid salt fuel can be continuously reprocessed online, eliminating India's spent fuel storage problem that has accumulated from Stage 1 PHWRs since the 1960s"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's Agni-5 ballistic missile achieving MIRV capability places India among a small group of nations. What is the strategic significance of MIRV technology specifically, beyond simply extending missile range?", opts: ["MIRV technology increases the missile's range to intercontinental distances by distributing the propulsion mass across multiple stages, allowing Agni-5 to reach targets across Eurasia and Africa, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital infrastructure choices have long-term geopolitical implications that","A MIRV-capable missile can deliver multiple warheads to geographically separate targets in a single launch, making missile defence systems far less effective since intercepting one missile no longer guarantees preventing all nuclear strikes - this fundamentally alters the deterrence calculus against adversaries with missile defence","MIRV capability certifies India's missiles under MTCR (Missile Technology Control Regime) standards, enabling India to export missile technology to friendly nations under civilian space cooperation agreements, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export","MIRVed missiles carry individually guided conventional warheads rather than nuclear warheads, making them legally permissible under the NPT while providing precise strike capability against hardened military targets, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's contribution of the cryostat to the ITER fusion project is described as one of India's most complex manufacturing achievements. Which statement best captures why fusion energy is considered a long-term solution to energy security rather than just another power source?", opts: ["Fusion reactors can be built at small scale like rooftop solar panels, allowing decentralised energy production that eliminates grid infrastructure requirements for rural electrification in countries like India, which reflects the Survey's broader argument about balancing short-term efficiency costs against long-term strategic resilience in sectors where supply chain concentration creates asymmetric vulnerability","Fusion fuel (deuterium from seawater, tritium bred from lithium) is effectively inexhaustible compared to finite uranium and coal reserves, produces no carbon emissions, generates no long-lived radioactive waste unlike fission, and carries no risk of runaway chain reactions - making it the only energy source that could simultaneously address climate change, energy security, and intergenerational resource equity","ITER will generate electricity commercially by 2035, replacing all fossil fuel plants within 20 years and making India energy-independent since it would only need to import lithium which is abundantly available in India's newly discovered Himalayan deposits, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Fusion reactors have already achieved net energy gain in laboratory conditions at NIF (National Ignition Facility) in California, proving the technology is ready for commercial deployment and India's ITER investment will return direct dividends within the next decade, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "India Defence: Operation Sindoor, Air Defence Systems and Indigenous Weapons",
    text: "Operation Sindoor demonstrated the operational maturity of India's layered air defence architecture. The integrated system successfully intercepted drone and missile threats on India's western border using a combination of Russian-supplied S-400 Triumf, jointly developed Barak-8, and indigenous Akash weapon systems. The Akashteer system, India's fully indigenous automated Air Defence Control and Reporting System, coordinated the integrated response by automatically detecting and engaging incoming aerial threats. India's DRDO simultaneously advanced multiple indigenous weapons programmes. The Scramjet engine achieved over 1000 seconds of active cooled combustion ground testing, a milestone towards hypersonic weapon capability. The AMCA fifth-generation fighter aircraft programme secured joint engine manufacturing approval with French company Safran. India's Bhargavastra anti-drone system uses a two-layer architecture: unguided micro-rockets against swarm drones and guided micro-missiles for precision strikes. The MK-II (A) laser-based Directed Energy Weapon system was tested at the National Open-Air Range in Andhra Pradesh.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["India's layered air defence architecture - S-400, Barak-8, Akash and Akashteer", "S-400 Triumf (Sudarshan Chakra in India): Russian long-range SAM; 360-degree radar and missile coverage; multi-missile compatibility for layered defence; phased array radar and electronic warfare countermeasures; range 40-400 km. Barak-8 (MR-SAM/LR-SAM): Jointly developed India-Israel; medium to long range; can simultaneously engage multiple aerial targets; maritime and land variants exist; developed by DRDO and Israel Aerospace Industries. Akash: Fully indigenous short-range SAM; Electronic Counter-Counter Measures (ECCM) for high jamming immunity; group and autonomous engagement modes. Akashteer: India's indigenous automated Air Defence Control and Reporting System - automatically detects, tracks, and assigns weapons to incoming aerial threats without manual intervention; played key role in Operation Sindoor by neutralising drone and missile threats. Layered defence logic: Different systems cover different ranges and altitudes - Akash handles short range, Barak-8 handles medium range, S-400 handles long range. UPSC GS3: indigenisation of defence, Operation Sindoor significance, air defence architecture."],
      ["Scramjet Engine, Hypersonic Weapons and AMCA fifth-generation fighter", "Scramjet (Supersonic Combusting Ramjet) engine: Burns fuel at supersonic speeds inside combustion chamber; improvement over ramjet (subsonic combustion). Key challenge: Flame stabilisation at supersonic internal airflow - DRDL achieved 1000+ seconds of active cooled combustor testing. Cannot produce thrust from zero speed - needs rocket assist to reach operational Mach number (typically Mach 4-5 before scramjet ignites). Advantage over rockets: Air-breathing so carries only fuel not oxidiser, greatly reducing weight. Hypersonic weapons: Mach 5 or faster; can manoeuvre unlike ballistic missiles making them extremely difficult to intercept. India joins USA, Russia, China with scramjet capability. AMCA (Advanced Medium Combat Aircraft): India's first 5th generation stealth fighter; features AI integration, internal weapons bays, super-cruise (supersonic without afterburner), low radar cross-section; joint engine manufacturing with Safran (France) for the M-88 derivative engine. 6th generation characteristics: AI integration, hypersonic capabilities, optional unmanned mode; China flew J-36 and J-50 prototypes. GSAT-7 series: Military communication satellites - GSAT-7 Rukmini (Navy), GSAT-7A Angry Bird (IAF), GSAT-7B (Army, under development), GSAT-7C (IAF ground hubs, under development). UPSC: Defence indigenisation, hypersonic technology, DRDO milestones."],
      ["Bhargavastra, DEW systems, Gaurav Glide Bomb and fibre optic drones", "Bhargavastra anti-drone system: Low-cost hard-kill system against drone swarms; Layer 1 uses unguided micro-rockets with 20m lethal radius for swarm suppression; Layer 2 uses guided micro-missiles for precision engagement of individual drones. Designed specifically to counter the threat demonstrated in Russia-Ukraine conflict where cheap drones overwhelmed expensive point-defence systems. Gaurav Long-Range Glide Bomb: 1,000 kg class; 100 km range; hybrid INS (Inertial Navigation System) and GPS guidance for pinpoint accuracy; after release, glides to target without propulsion making it difficult to detect. MK-II (A) Directed Energy Weapon: Laser weapon tested at National Open-Air Range (NOAR), Andhra Pradesh; developed by DRDO's CHESS (Centre for High Energy Systems and Sciences), Hyderabad; engages targets detected by radar or Electro-Optic system; India joined USA, China, Russia with advanced laser weapon capability; Israel's Iron Beam first combat-proven laser system. Fibre Optic Drones: Resistant to electronic warfare (EW) jamming because guidance signal travels through physical fibre cable rather than radio frequency - radio jamming cannot affect them; demonstrated as game-changer in Russia-Ukraine conflict. Rudrastra: Hybrid VTOL (Vertical Take-Off and Landing) UAV; can take off without runway; equipped with precision-guided warheads; automated return capability. UPSC: Drone warfare, DEW technology, anti-drone systems."],
      ["Project Kusha, IADWS and India's indigenous missile defence development", "Project Kusha (Program for Long Range Surface to Air Missile / PGLRSAM): Indigenous long-range air defence missile system being developed by DRDO. Phase 1: Four interceptor variants M1, M2, M3 and naval variant under development. Phase 2: Range exceeding 600 km, targeting ballistic missiles in their terminal and midcourse phases. Anant Shastra: Indigenous Quick Reaction Surface-to-Air Missile (QRSAM) system; contract awarded to BEL (Bharat Electronics Limited) for development. IADWS (Indigenous Air Defence Weapon System): Centralised Command and Control Centre by DRDL. Components include QRSAM for mobile armour column protection, Advanced VSHORADS (Very Short-Range Air Defence System) by Research Centre Imarat for low-altitude threats, and high-power laser DEW. Netra MkII: Next-generation Airborne Early Warning and Control System (AWACS); mounted on Airbus A321 platform; detects and tracks threats across vast distances; functions as flying command centre. Stratospheric Airship Platform (HAPS): Operates 17-22 km altitude; uses helium for buoyancy; solar powered; bridges gap between drones and satellites for border surveillance, disaster response, and intelligence missions. UPSC: DRDO programmes, defence indigenisation timeline, air defence layers."],
      ["Critical Minerals and India's defence and technology supply chain", "Critical Minerals: Naturally occurring elements or compounds with high demand and supply risks; India's Ministry of Mines lists 30 critical minerals including lithium, cobalt, nickel, rare earth elements (REEs), gallium, germanium, graphite. REEs (Rare Earth Elements): 17 elements; used in permanent magnets (wind turbines, EV motors), defence radar and sonar, electronics phosphors, industrial catalysts; China controls 85% of global supply chain and over two-thirds of production. India's REE reserves: Mostly in monazite sands of coastal Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh, Odisha. National Critical Mineral Mission (NCMM): India's response to supply chain vulnerability. Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Amendment Act 2023 enables exploration and mining of critical minerals. Quad Critical Minerals Initiative: USA, Japan, India, Australia coordinating to reduce China dependence. Defence relevance: Gallium and germanium (semiconductor manufacturing, night vision); cobalt (aircraft engine alloys); REE magnets (missile guidance, radar). UPSC GS3: Critical minerals policy, supply chain security, India-China strategic competition."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "The Akashteer system played a central role in Operation Sindoor's air defence response. What distinguishes Akashteer from the individual missile systems like S-400, Barak-8 and Akash that it coordinates?", opts: ["Akashteer is a new missile system with longer range than S-400 that was deployed for the first time during Operation Sindoor, filling a gap in India's high-altitude interception capability, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Akashteer is India's indigenous automated Air Defence Control and Reporting System that integrates data from radars and assigns engagement tasks to the appropriate weapon system without manual intervention - it is the command-and-control brain that makes the separate missile systems work as a coherent layered network rather than as isolated point defences","Akashteer is the export version of the Akash missile system jointly manufactured with Russia and Israel, combining features of all three systems in a single platform for cost-effective air defence, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Akashteer provides electronic warfare jamming capability that blinds incoming missiles' guidance systems before the Akash and Barak-8 missiles intercept them physically, serving as the first layer of a six-layer defensive architecture, an institutional complementarity the Survey identifies when explaining why sector-specific interventions like PLI need to be paired with cross-cutting"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's Scramjet engine programme achieving 1000+ seconds of active cooled combustion testing is described as a milestone towards hypersonic weapons. Why does achieving sustained combustion specifically represent the core engineering challenge in scramjet development?", opts: ["The 1000-second test certified the thermal management system that keeps the scramjet combustor walls from ablating at sustained supersonic combustion temperatures, demonstrating that India has solved both the combustion stability and structural durability challenges that have caused failures in other nations' hypersonic programmes at this testing stage","Sustaining combustion inside a scramjet means maintaining a stable flame in an airflow moving at multiple times the speed of sound - this is extremely difficult because the fuel-air mixture spends only milliseconds in the combustor, requiring precise fuel injection and flame stabilisation technology; solving this is the central engineering barrier between laboratory demonstrations and operational hypersonic weapons","The 1000-second test certified the thermal protection system that keeps the engine body from melting during hypersonic flight, which is the only technical requirement remaining before India can begin mass production of hypersonic missiles for the Air Force","Achieving 1000 seconds of testing means India's scramjet engine meets the MTCR (Missile Technology Control Regime) minimum duration threshold required for classified weapons export licences to allied nations, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Bhargavastra's two-layer design (unguided micro-rockets + guided micro-missiles) specifically addresses a vulnerability exposed by the Russia-Ukraine conflict. What does this vulnerability reveal about conventional point-defence systems against drone warfare?", opts: ["Conventional systems use radar frequencies that drones can detect and avoid, so Bhargavastra uses acoustic sensors instead, which drones cannot detect or jam, making the system effective against all drone types including stealth UAVs, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning","Cheap drone swarms can overwhelm conventional missile-based point defences because each interception consumes an expensive missile - Bhargavastra's unguided rockets address swarm volume at low cost per kill while guided missiles handle precision targets, matching the cost asymmetry that makes drone warfare economically advantageous for attackers","The Ukraine conflict showed drones fly below conventional radar minimum detection altitude, so Bhargavastra's guided micro-missiles are designed specifically for sub-50 metre altitude engagement where S-400 and Barak-8 cannot engage targets, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern","Conventional air defence requires operator authorisation for each engagement, creating dangerous delays against fast drones; Bhargavastra's innovation is fully autonomous target selection without human-in-the-loop, making it legally classified as a lethal autonomous weapons system under international humanitarian law, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "China's dominance controlling 85% of rare earth element supply chains is identified as a strategic vulnerability for India's defence and technology sectors. Which aspect of REE properties makes substitution or alternative sourcing particularly difficult in the near term?", opts: ["REEs are radioactive materials subject to strict international safeguards under the IAEA, making it illegal for most countries to establish domestic processing facilities without Chinese technical assistance, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from geographic","REEs have unique magnetic, phosphorescent and catalytic properties that arise from their specific electron configurations - no other materials provide the same performance in applications like permanent magnets for missile guidance, radar systems and EV motors, making them genuinely difficult to replace rather than merely expensive to source elsewhere","The processing of REE ores produces toxic thorium as a by-product, and India's nuclear regulations prevent domestic REE refining near the coastal monazite deposits where India's reserves are concentrated, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain resilience,","REEs are critical only for civilian electronics manufacturing, not for defence systems, so China's supply chain dominance affects India's consumer electronics industry but does not create direct defence vulnerabilities, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Fibre optic drones demonstrated in the Russia-Ukraine conflict are described as resistant to electronic warfare jamming. What physical property of fibre optic guidance makes jamming ineffective against these drones specifically?", opts: ["Fibre optic cables transmit guidance signals using infrared light at frequencies beyond the range of current Russian and Ukrainian electronic warfare systems, which operate only in radio frequency bands below 40 GHz, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale","Guidance signals in fibre optic drones travel as pulses of light through a physical glass cable connecting the drone to its operator - radio frequency jamming cannot affect light pulses travelling inside a closed physical medium, making electronic warfare countermeasures that work by flooding the radio spectrum completely irrelevant against this guidance method","Fibre optic cables are coated with radar-absorbing material that also blocks electromagnetic interference, giving the drones stealth properties that prevent both radar detection and electronic jamming simultaneously, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning curve dynamics and permanent","Fibre optic guidance uses quantum entanglement to transmit control signals instantaneously regardless of distance or intervening jamming fields, representing an application of quantum communication technology developed originally for satellite cryptography, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "Health: Tuberculosis, Antimicrobial Resistance, Vaccines and Diabetes Types",
    text: "India's TB incidence reduced by 21 percent according to WHO's Global TB Report 2025, reflecting sustained progress under the National TB Elimination Programme targeting elimination by 2025. Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, spreads through airborne droplets from pulmonary cases, and is categorised by site of infection into pulmonary and extrapulmonary types, and by drug response into drug-sensitive, multi-drug resistant and extensively drug-resistant forms. Separately, malnutrition-related diabetes was formally recognised as Type 5 diabetes at the International Diabetes Federation World Congress, joining the existing classification of Type 1 autoimmune, Type 2 lifestyle-related, and gestational diabetes. The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2025 was awarded for discoveries concerning peripheral immune tolerance, specifically the role of the FOXP3 gene in producing regulatory T cells that prevent autoimmune attacks. India also licensed production of AdFalciVax, its first indigenous multi-stage malaria vaccine targeting both the liver stage and the sexual transmission stage of Plasmodium falciparum, unlike existing single-stage vaccines.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["Tuberculosis - classification, drug resistance and India's elimination programme", "TB caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Transmission: Airborne droplets from pulmonary TB patients when coughing, sneezing, speaking - only pulmonary TB is contagious in this way. Classification by site: Pulmonary TB (lungs, most common), Extrapulmonary TB (lymph nodes, bones, brain, kidneys, pleura), Spinal TB or Pott's Disease (spine, causes severe back pain). Classification by drug response: Drug-Sensitive TB (responds to first-line drugs isoniazid and rifampicin); MDR-TB (Multi-Drug Resistant - does not respond to isoniazid AND rifampicin); XDR-TB (Extensively Drug Resistant - also resistant to second-line fluoroquinolones and injectable agents). National TB Elimination Programme: Target elimination (less than 1 case per million) by 2025, five years ahead of global Sustainable Development Goal target of 2030. 21% reduction in incidence per WHO 2025 report. NIKSHAY portal: Digital platform for TB notification and patient management. Treatment: DOTS (Directly Observed Treatment Short-course). India has world's highest TB burden (about 26% of global cases) despite significant progress. UPSC: Public health, communicable disease policy, SDG health targets."],
      ["Nobel Prize 2025 - Peripheral Immune Tolerance and FOXP3 gene", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2025: Awarded to Mary E. Brunkow, Fred Ramsdell and Shimon Sakaguchi for discoveries on peripheral immune tolerance. Immune tolerance concept: Immune system must attack pathogens but not the body's own cells - this discrimination is called self-tolerance. Central tolerance: Occurs in the thymus gland (lymphatic system organ that trains T cells). Thymus eliminates self-reactive T cells (those that would attack the body) during development. However, some self-reactive T cells escape into circulation - these could cause autoimmune disease. Peripheral tolerance: Additional control mechanism using Regulatory T cells (Treg cells). Treg cells act as immune security guards - they identify escaped self-reactive T cells and suppress their activity. FOXP3 gene (discovered by Brunkow and Ramsdell): Master transcription factor that programs T cells to become Treg cells. FOXP3 mutations cause IPEX syndrome (rare severe autoimmune disease affecting multiple organs). Shimon Sakaguchi: Identified the specific surface marker (CD25) that characterises Treg cells, making them identifiable and studyable. Implications: Opens paths to treating autoimmune diseases (Type 1 diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis), preventing organ rejection in transplants, and improving cancer immunotherapy by managing Treg suppression of anti-tumour immunity. UPSC: Nobel Prizes, immunology, autoimmune diseases."],
      ["AdFalciVax and India's malaria vaccine development", "Malaria: Life-threatening disease caused by Plasmodium parasites transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes. Five species cause human malaria: P. falciparum (deadliest), P. vivax, P. malariae, P. ovale, P. knowlesi. AdFalciVax: India's first indigenous multi-stage malaria vaccine. Developed by: ICMR's Regional Medical Research Centre, Bhubaneswar and National Institute of Malaria Research in partnership with DBT-National Institute of Immunology. What makes it different: Existing vaccines (RTS,S/AS01 called Mosquirix, and R21/Matrix-M) target only the pre-erythrocytic (liver) stage. AdFalciVax targets TWO stages: (1) Pre-erythrocytic liver stage - prevents parasite establishing in liver; (2) Transmission/sexual stage - reduces parasite spread back to mosquitoes from infected humans. Chimeric vaccine design: Combines antigens from different parasite life cycle stages into a single immunogen. Strategic importance: Targeting the transmission stage could reduce community spread, not just protect the vaccinated individual. Government licensed private firms to produce AdFalciVax. UPSC: Malaria elimination, vaccine development, ICMR programmes."],
      ["Antimicrobial Resistance (AMR) - mechanism, spread and global response", "AMR: When bacteria, viruses, fungi or parasites evolve resistance to antimicrobial drugs. Natural process accelerated by anthropogenic misuse. Superbugs: Strains resistant to most or all available antimicrobials. AMR mechanism: Vertical gene transfer (during bacterial cell division, resistance genes passed to daughter cells) and Horizontal gene transfer (between different bacterial species via plasmids - resistance can jump between unrelated bacteria). WHO: Classified AMR as one of top ten global health threats; described as a silent pandemic. Contributing factors: Overuse of antibiotics in human medicine, agricultural use (growth promotion in livestock), inadequate sanitation enabling resistant bacteria to spread, poor infection prevention in hospitals. SHRESTH Initiative: State Health Regulatory Excellence Index launched by Union Health Ministry via CDSCO to benchmark state drug regulatory performance - ensures quality of medicines reaching patients. Fixed-Dose Combination (FDC) Drugs: Contain two or more active ingredients in fixed ratio; CDSCO directed states to stop 35 unapproved FDCs - reflects AMR concern (irrational combinations drive resistance) and drug safety oversight. India's WHO Pandemic Agreement commitment: Strengthens International Health Regulations 2005 compliance for pathogen access and benefit sharing. UPSC: AMR policy, drug regulation, pandemic preparedness."],
      ["Types of Diabetes and the Type 5 malnutrition-linked recognition", "Diabetes mellitus: Disorder of insulin - either insufficient production (pancreatic beta cell failure) or inadequate tissue response (insulin resistance). Type 1 Diabetes: Autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing pancreatic beta cells; typically onset in childhood; requires lifelong insulin injection; FOXP3/Treg research relevant - autoimmune mechanism. Type 2 Diabetes: Mainly caused by obesity, physical inactivity, poor diet; body does not use insulin effectively; most prevalent globally; manageable with lifestyle changes and oral drugs. Gestational Diabetes: High blood glucose during pregnancy; risks to both mother (later Type 2) and child (macrosomia, hypoglycaemia at birth). Type 5 Diabetes (new 2025 recognition): Also called J-type diabetes; linked to chronic undernutrition especially in childhood; different pathophysiology from Types 1 and 2; affects underweight individuals (low BMI) in contrast to Type 2 which affects overweight individuals. Formerly called Malnutrition-Related Diabetes Mellitus (MRDM) or Tropical Diabetes. Particularly relevant for India: Childhood malnutrition remains a public health challenge; Type 5 recognition helps direct appropriate treatment (high-calorie diet support rather than obesity interventions). Wegovy (semaglutide): GLP-1 receptor agonist weight loss drug by Novo Nordisk launched in India in injection form; effective for Type 2 but inappropriate for Type 5. UPSC: NCD policy, malnutrition, diabetes epidemiology in India."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "India's target of TB elimination by 2025 is five years ahead of the global SDG target of 2030. The WHO report showing 21% reduction in incidence is progress towards elimination, but elimination is defined as below one case per million population. Which aspect of India's TB burden makes achieving elimination significantly harder than merely reducing incidence?", opts: ["India's 21 percent incidence reduction reflects improved DOTS treatment completion rates and active case finding through Ni-kshay Mitra patient support programmes, and the challenge now is that remaining cases are disproportionately concentrated among high-risk groups including people living with HIV, diabetics and underweight individuals where standard first-line treatment regimens have lower success rates","India carries approximately 26% of the global TB burden while having only 17% of the global population, and the co-existence of drug-resistant TB strains (MDR-TB and XDR-TB) alongside high HIV co-infection rates and malnutrition creates a complex epidemiological environment where standard DOTS-based programmes are insufficient to drive incidence to elimination levels","The National TB Elimination Programme is funded predominantly by the World Bank and faces disbursement delays that periodically disrupt medicine supply chains, creating treatment interruptions that generate drug resistance, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from geographic","TB elimination requires achieving vaccine-induced herd immunity, but India's BCG vaccination programme provides only partial protection against pulmonary TB in adults and no protection against drug-resistant strains, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning curve dynamics and permanent protective"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Nobel Prize in Physiology 2025 recognised the discovery of FOXP3's role in regulatory T cells. How does this discovery connect to the challenge of cancer immunotherapy, beyond its obvious relevance to autoimmune diseases?", opts: ["FOXP3 mutations directly cause several forms of cancer by disabling the immune system's ability to detect and eliminate early tumour cells, meaning gene therapy to restore FOXP3 function would be a universal cancer treatment, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers, a technology sovereignty consideration","Treg cells suppress immune responses broadly to prevent autoimmunity - but within tumours, the same Treg activity suppresses the anti-tumour immune response, allowing cancers to evade immune surveillance; understanding FOXP3 and Treg biology opens the possibility of selectively reducing Treg activity within tumour environments to unleash immune-mediated cancer killing without triggering systemic autoimmunity","FOXP3 regulatory T cells produce interferon-gamma which directly kills cancer cells through a mechanism distinct from conventional chemotherapy, providing a molecular basis for developing biological cancer treatments with fewer side effects than current treatments, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale","The FOXP3 gene is located on the X chromosome, explaining why autoimmune diseases and certain cancers disproportionately affect women, and this sex-linked inheritance pattern has direct implications for gender-specific cancer screening protocols in India, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "AdFalciVax's design targeting both the pre-erythrocytic liver stage AND the sexual transmission stage of P. falciparum represents a different strategy from existing malaria vaccines. What is the public health significance of targeting the transmission stage specifically?", opts: ["Targeting the transmission stage ensures the vaccine provides lifelong immunity rather than the 18-month protection window of existing single-stage vaccines like RTS,S, making it more cost-effective for India's immunisation programme","A vaccine that reduces parasite transmission from humans back to mosquitoes protects non-vaccinated community members as well as vaccinated individuals - this transmission-blocking effect could reduce community malaria burden even in populations with incomplete vaccination coverage, providing a tool for malaria elimination rather than just individual protection","The sexual stage targeting means AdFalciVax can be given to infected individuals after they develop symptoms, treating the active infection rather than just preventing it, giving India a therapeutic vaccine option that current preventive-only malaria vaccines cannot provide, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","Plasmodium falciparum's sexual stage occurs in the bloodstream rather than the liver, and targeting transmission stage antigens has been less successful in earlier vaccine trials because blood-stage immunity tends to be less durable than liver-stage immunity in naturally exposed populations"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Type 5 diabetes being formally recognised as a distinct category is significant for India specifically. Why would applying Type 2 diabetes management protocols (designed for overweight, insulin-resistant patients) to Type 5 patients be inappropriate or harmful?", opts: ["Type 5 diabetes is caused by the same autoimmune mechanism as Type 1, so Type 2 management protocols using oral metformin would be ineffective and would delay appropriate insulin therapy, causing preventable beta cell exhaustion, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","Type 5 diabetes is linked to chronic undernutrition causing inadequate pancreatic development - patients are typically underweight with insufficient caloric intake; prescribing Type 2 interventions like caloric restriction, exercise intensification and weight loss drugs (such as semaglutide) would worsen the underlying nutritional deficit that caused the condition, potentially accelerating organ dysfunction","Type 2 management requires expensive continuous glucose monitors and insulin pumps that are too costly for the rural populations where Type 5 is most common, so the recognition is primarily about making cheaper treatment options reimbursable under government health insurance schemes","Type 5 patients have distinct pancreatic morphology from chronic undernutrition with smaller beta cell mass than Type 1 or Type 2 patients, and optimal management requires high-calorie nutritional rehabilitation alongside carefully titrated insulin supplementation rather than the oral hypoglycaemic agents that form the first-line treatment for Type 2 diabetes"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Horizontal gene transfer enabling AMR genes to jump between unrelated bacterial species represents why antimicrobial resistance spreads faster than would be expected from simple mutation and inheritance. Which implication of this mechanism is most significant for India's antibiotic stewardship policy?", opts: ["Horizontal gene transfer only occurs between bacteria of the same species in water sources, meaning India's priority should be purifying drinking water rather than regulating antibiotic prescriptions in healthcare settings, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","Because resistance genes can transfer from harmless environmental bacteria to dangerous pathogens through plasmid exchange, antibiotic use in agriculture (as growth promoters in livestock) can select for resistance genes in gut flora that subsequently transfer to pathogenic bacteria causing human infections - making agricultural antibiotic regulation as important as clinical prescription control","Horizontal gene transfer has been demonstrated to occur only between aerobic bacteria, so India's anaerobic sewage treatment systems provide a natural barrier that prevents resistance genes from accumulating in urban water supplies, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","AMR genes transferred horizontally tend to impose metabolic costs on recipient bacteria, but in environments with continuous antibiotic pressure these fitness costs are quickly compensated by counter-evolutionary adaptations that make horizontally acquired resistance clinically similar in severity to mutational resistance, a technology sovereignty consideration the Survey raises when arguing that India's digital"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "Ancient Ports, Buddhist Relics, Jainism and Maritime Heritage of India",
    text: "India's maritime heritage spans millennia. The Piprahwa Relics, discovered in 1898 in Siddharthnagar, Uttar Pradesh, were identified through a Brahmi script inscription as bone fragments of the Buddha deposited by the Sakya clan, dating to the third century BCE. Attempts to auction these relics in Hong Kong were halted after India intervened, citing their classification as AA antiquities under Indian law. Ancient ports like Muzuris on the Malabar Coast of Kerala and Poompuhar at the mouth of the Kaveri River in Tamil Nadu were major nodes in the Indian Ocean trading network, connecting India to the Roman Empire and Southeast Asia. INSV Kaundinya, built using the ancient Tankai stitched-plank shipbuilding method depicted in Ajanta cave paintings, completed a maiden voyage from Porbandar to Muscat, demonstrating living continuity of India's maritime traditions. UNESCO launched the world's first Virtual Museum of Stolen Cultural Objects at MONDIACULT 2025, featuring notable Indian artifacts including two ninth-century Nataraja and Bhairava sandstone sculptures from Chhattisgarh.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["Piprahwa Relics - discovery, significance and India's cultural property claims", "Piprahwa Relics: Discovered 1898 by British civil engineer William Claxton Peppé in Piprahwa, Siddharthnagar, Uttar Pradesh. Location identified with ancient Kapilavastu, the Sakya capital where the historical Buddha grew up. Key contents: Bone fragments believed to be Buddha's mortal remains (body relics), soapstone and crystal caskets, sandstone coffer, gold ornaments. Dating: Enshrined approximately 3rd century BCE. Authentication: Brahmi script inscription on casket reads that these are relics deposited by the Sakya clan - direct epigraphic evidence of relic identity. Eight original stupas: Built to distribute Buddha's cremated remains - Rajagriha, Vaishali, Allakappa, Ramagrama, Pava, Kushinagar, Vethapida, and Kapilavastu (Piprahwa). Legal status in India: Classified as 'AA' antiquities under Indian law - highest protection category prohibiting removal, sale, or export. Current issue: Attempted auction in Hong Kong in 2025 was halted. Relic veneration in Buddhism: Body relics (Sarira) have been objects of pilgrimage and devotion since the Parinirvana of the Buddha; stupas were originally relic repositories. Other important relic sites: Vaishali (Bihar), Devni Mori (Gujarat), Nagarjunakonda (Andhra Pradesh). UPSC: Buddhist archaeology, cultural property protection, Brahmi script."],
      ["Ancient Maritime Ports - Muzuris, Poompuhar and India-Rome trade", "Muzuris (Muchiri): Located on Malabar Coast, present-day Kerala. Active from 1st century BCE through early medieval period. Major exports: Black pepper (called black gold), semi-precious stones, ivory, pearls, cotton textiles, spices. Connected India with Persia, Middle East, North Africa, and Mediterranean including Greek and Roman traders. Mentioned in Pliny the Elder's 'Naturalis Historia' and in the 'Periplus of the Erythraean Sea' (anonymous 1st century CE Greek merchant manual). Roman coins (gold aureus and silver denarius) have been found throughout Kerala coastal regions confirming the trade. Poompuhar (Kaveripattinam): Ancient Chola port at mouth of Kaveri River, Tamil Nadu; second capital of early Cholas. Exports: Pearls, spices, textiles. Tamil epic 'Manimekalai' (by Sattanar, Sangam period) records its submergence under the sea - confirmed by underwater archaeological finds. Periplus of the Erythraean Sea: 1st century CE Greek text providing the most detailed account of Indian Ocean trade routes of the ancient period. India's export economy: Pepper, muslin, indigo, steel (wootz), teakwood for Roman shipbuilding. UPSC: Ancient Indian economy, trade routes, Sangam literature, Roman connections."],
      ["INSV Kaundinya, Tankai Method and India's ancient shipbuilding traditions", "INSV Kaundinya: Indian Naval Sailing Vessel reconstructed using the Tankai (also called the sewn-plank or stitched-plank) method. Based on 5th century CE ship depicted in Ajanta Cave paintings. Named after the legendary Indian mariner Kaundinya who sailed to Southeast Asia, founding a dynasty in what is now Cambodia - evidence of early Indian maritime expansion. Tankai method: Planks fastened by stitching with coir (coconut fibre rope) rather than metal nails; the flexibility of stitched joints absorbs wave impacts better than rigid nailed construction; caulked with natural resins and oils; this technique allowed vessels to survive monsoon ocean crossings. Voyage: Maiden overseas voyage from Porbandar, Gujarat (Mahatma Gandhi's birthplace) to Muscat, Sultanate of Oman - retracing ancient Indian Ocean trade routes. Maritime significance timeline: Magadha navy (Arthashastra mentions naval warfare department); Cholas and Cheras mastered monsoon winds for sea voyages; Rajendra Chola I conquered Srivijaya Empire (present-day Malaysia/Indonesia, ruled by Sailendra dynasty); Chhatrapati Shivaji built coastal forts and over 500-ship navy. Ajanta paintings: Depict Bodhisattvas, Jataka tales, royal courts; Buddhist art tradition; the ship painting provides direct evidence of ancient shipbuilding technology. UPSC: Maritime history, Ajanta caves, Indian Ocean trade networks."],
      ["Jainism - Navkar Mahamantra, Santhara and Agamas", "Jainism: One of India's oldest shramana traditions; founded or revived by Vardhamana Mahavira (24th Tirthankara, 599-527 BCE); rejects Vedic authority; emphasises ahimsa (non-violence), satya (truth), aparigraha (non-possessiveness). Navkar Mahamantra: Most sacred chant in Jainism; pays respects to five categories: Arihant (those who attained Keval Gyan or perfect knowledge), Siddhas (who eradicated eight karmas and attained Moksha), Acharya (senior monks following Mahavrat), Upadhyaya (teacher monks), Sadhus (all monks). It does not invoke a personal God but reveres the qualities of enlightened souls. Santhara (Sallekhana): Jain religious practice of voluntarily fasting unto death when death is near or religious duties can no longer be performed due to old age, incurable illness, or extreme conditions. Types: Trivihar (gives up food but not water), Chauvihar (gives up both food and water). Legal controversy: Rajasthan HC (2015) ruled it illegal under IPC Section 306 (abetment of suicide); later stayed by Supreme Court - question of whether religious freedom protects it. Agamas: Post-Vedic Jain scriptures conveying ritual knowledge; temple management manuals; deal with Jnana (knowledge), Yoga (meditation), Kriya (rituals), Charya (worship); three divisions - Vaishnava, Saiva, Sakta agamas. Important texts: Isvara-Samhita, Narada-Pancharatra, Spanda-Pradipika, Mahanirvana-Tantra. UPSC: Jain philosophy, religious freedom, SC constitutional questions."],
      ["Cultural diffusion through maritime trade - Buddhism, Hinduism and Southeast Asia", "India-Southeast Asia connections: Ancient Indian maritime trade created some of history's most consequential cultural transmissions. Borobudur temple, Java (Indonesia): World's largest Buddhist monument, 9th century CE, Sailendra dynasty who also maintained maritime connections with Pala dynasty Bengal and Nalanda. Angkor Wat, Cambodia: 12th century CE Hindu temple complex built by Khmer Empire; originally dedicated to Vishnu; reflects Indian architectural and religious influence carried by maritime traders. Kaundinya legend: Indian Brahmin who sailed to Southeast Asia and married the Naga princess Soma, founding a dynasty in Funan (Cambodia/Vietnam) - personifies the process of Indian cultural influence in Southeast Asia. Buddhism spread eastward: Ashoka's son Mahendra and daughter Sanghamitra sailed from Tamralipti (Bengal) to Ceylon/Sri Lanka to spread Buddhism. Rajendra Chola I (1014-1044 CE): Naval expeditions against Srivijaya Empire, a Buddhist maritime trading state based in Sumatra/Malaya that had previously blocked Indian Ocean trade routes. Chola-Pala maritime diplomacy: Sailendra dynasty sent embassies to Pala court requesting permission to build a monastery at Nalanda. Sri Lanka's Meghavarna ruler sent to Samudragupta seeking permission for a Buddhist temple at Gaya. Virtual Museum of Stolen Cultural Objects (UNESCO, 2025): Digital platform at MONDIACULT 2025 highlighting illicit trafficking; Indian artefacts include 9th century Nataraja and Bhairava sandstone sculptures from Pali, Chhattisgarh. UPSC: Indian Ocean trade history, cultural diffusion, Southeast Asian Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "The Piprahwa Relics' classification as 'AA' antiquities under Indian law was invoked to halt their auction in Hong Kong. What legal and cultural logic underlies India's claim to these objects specifically, as distinct from a general claim on all Buddhist artifacts?", opts: ["India's claim on the Piprahwa Relics rests on their provenance from Kapilavastu in present-day Uttar Pradesh, their AA antiquity classification under the Antiquities and Art Treasures Act 1972 which prohibits export, and the argument that bones of a person born and raised in the territory that is now India carry a patrimonial claim independent of the UNESCO 1970 Convention's documentation requirements","The relics carry a Brahmi inscription specifically identifying them as Buddha's remains deposited by the Sakya clan at Kapilavastu - a site identified as being in present-day Uttar Pradesh, India - creating both an epigraphic chain of custody linking them directly to Indian territory and their classification as the highest protected category under Indian antiquity law, which prohibits sale and removal from the country","India's claim is diplomatic rather than legal - since the relics were removed by a British civil servant during colonial occupation, India can seek their return under post-colonial repatriation norms similar to the Kohinoor diamond claim, but has no legal standing under international property law","India invokes the UNESCO 1970 Convention on cultural property alongside the domestic Antiquities and Art Treasures Act 1972, arguing that relics removed from colonial India by a British colonial official in 1898 constitute illicit export under retrospective application of cultural property protection principles that reflect customary international law obligations predating the formal convention"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea describes ancient Muzuris as a major port while Roman coins have been found throughout Kerala. What does this combination of literary and archaeological evidence collectively reveal about ancient India's economic integration with the Roman world?", opts: ["It suggests that Roman merchants permanently settled in Kerala coastal cities, leading to a creolised Indo-Roman culture whose descendants remain identifiable through genetic studies of present-day Kerala Christian communities, which reflects the Survey's broader argument about balancing short-term efficiency costs against long-term strategic resilience in sectors where supply chain concentration creates asymmetric vulnerability","Literary description plus numismatic evidence shows Muzuris was not merely a transit point but a destination where direct monetary transactions occurred between Indian and Roman traders - Rome's chronic trade deficit with India (paying in gold for pepper, luxury goods) is recorded by Pliny as draining Roman treasury, confirming India's active role in the early globalised economy rather than passive reception of foreign commerce","The Roman coins found in Kerala were used as ballast in Roman merchant ships rather than for trade, suggesting Roman ships visiting Muzuris were actually in distress rather than engaged in systematic commercial activity, though this view understates the institutional constraints that have historically limited the effectiveness of market-based solutions in sectors with significant information asymmetries and coordination failures","Periplus and numismatic evidence together demonstrate that Roman military garrisons were stationed at Muzuris to protect the spice trade route, similar to the later Portuguese Estado da India, suggesting a pattern of foreign military presence preceding colonial control"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "INSV Kaundinya's use of the Tankai stitched-plank method rather than nail-fastened construction reflects a specific engineering principle that made it suitable for ancient Indian Ocean voyages. What was this structural advantage?", opts: ["Stitching with coir created a watertight seal superior to metal nails because coir swells when wet, plugging any microscopic gaps between planks more effectively than the oakum caulking used in European nail-fastened shipbuilding of the same era, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","The flexibility of stitched joints allows the hull to flex and absorb wave impacts without cracking - in rough monsoon seas, a rigid nail-fastened hull transfers wave energy as shock to the entire structure, while a sewn hull distributes stress across the flexible joints, making stitched vessels both stronger in practice and repairable at sea using available natural materials without metalworking facilities","Coir stitching is lighter than iron nails, reducing displacement and allowing ancient Indian ships to carry larger cargo volumes of pepper and textiles than would have been possible with heavier metal-fastened European-style construction, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","The Tankai method was chosen not for engineering advantages but because it preserved the exact specifications recorded in the Ajanta paintings, ensuring historical authenticity of the reconstruction project and qualifying it for UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage recognition as living maritime tradition, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Rajasthan High Court's 2015 ruling that Santhara (Jain fasting unto death) constitutes abetment of suicide under IPC Section 306 was stayed by the Supreme Court. What is the fundamental constitutional tension that makes this case difficult to resolve?", opts: ["The case is between Article 25 (freedom of religion) and the Jain community's claim that Santhara is not a religious practice but a medical decision equivalent to passive euthanasia, making it a healthcare regulation rather than a religious freedom question, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale needed for technological learning while maintaining the competitive pressure needed for efficiency","Article 25 (right to freedom of religion) protects religious practices that are essential to a religion's identity, and Jains argue Santhara has been practised since Jainism's founding as a spiritually meaningful conscious choice - but the state's parens patriae duty to protect life under Article 21 creates a counter-claim that the state must prevent death regardless of religious motivation, raising whether voluntary religious self-imposed death can be simultaneously a protected religious act and a criminally prosecutable suicide","The case turns on whether Jainism qualifies as a religion under Article 25, since the court must first determine if Jainism's rejection of a personal creator God disqualifies it from constitutional religious protections that were designed with theistic traditions in mind, a measurement limitation the Survey acknowledges when arguing that conventional GDP and trade statistics do not capture the full strategic value of supply chain resilience, making cost-benefit analysis of indigenisation systematically biased toward underinvestment","Santhara becomes legally problematic only when observed by minors who cannot give informed consent, and the Supreme Court's stay is specifically to allow time to draft age-restriction regulations rather than to determine whether adult Santhara is constitutionally protected, a historical precedent the Survey draws on when contextualising India's current industrial policy choices within the broader pattern of how successful late-developing economies have managed the tension between static efficiency and dynamic capability building"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's ancient maritime connections with Southeast Asia resulted in Hindu and Buddhist kingdoms like Angkor Wat and Borobudur adopting Indian religious and architectural traditions. Which factor most fundamentally explains why Indian cultural influence in Southeast Asia took the form of adoption rather than conquest or colonisation?", opts: ["Indian maritime traders and Brahmin priests deliberately avoided political conquest to preserve trading relationships, recognising that economic cooperation with independent Southeast Asian kingdoms was more profitable than the administrative costs of colonial control, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","Indian cultural transmission to Southeast Asia was primarily merchant and Brahmin-led rather than militarily imposed - local Southeast Asian elites selectively adopted Indian religious concepts, Sanskrit literacy, temple architecture and statecraft as prestige markers that legitimised their rule domestically, while Indian traders and ritual specialists benefited from patronage relationships; this voluntary adoption model created deep cultural integration without territorial control","Southeast Asian kingdoms adopted Indian culture because they were conquered by Indian dynasties starting with Kaundinya in the 1st century CE, and Angkor Wat and Borobudur were built by rulers of direct Indian descent who imposed their ancestral religion on the local population, which the Survey contrasts with India's actual policy record where administrative capacity constraints have consistently delayed the translation of sound policy design into effective programme implementation","Indian cultural influence in Southeast Asia was spread through both Buddhist monks and Brahmin advisors who served at Southeast Asian royal courts, and the non-violent framing of Buddhist transmission overlooks that Brahmin-led Hindu temple construction was equally pervasive and involved close integration with warrior-king political systems, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  },
  {
    topic: "Quantum Technology, Brain-Computer Interface and Emerging Technologies",
    text: "India's National Quantum Mission received a boost when QpiAI launched India's first 25-qubit superconducting quantum computer QpiAI-Indus on World Quantum Day, along with India's first quantum computing chip. Quantum Key Distribution was demonstrated over existing optical fibre infrastructure under the I-Hub Quantum Technology Foundation. The QSIP (Quantum Random Number Generator System in Package) was dedicated to the nation as a quantum security chip providing certified randomness for cryptographic applications. Stanford scientists developed a privacy-focused Brain-Computer Interface, while Neuralink aims to implant its Blindsight chip in a human to restore vision by bypassing the retina and stimulating the visual cortex directly. Japan built a 3D-printed train station in six hours demonstrating additive manufacturing at infrastructure scale. The Jevons Paradox and Solow Paradox were highlighted in discussions about AI energy consumption: the Jevons Paradox predicts that efficiency improvements increase rather than decrease total resource demand, while the Solow Paradox notes that technology adoption has not consistently translated to measurable productivity gains.",
    keyFacts: [
      ["National Quantum Mission and India's quantum technology ecosystem", "National Quantum Mission (NQM): One of nine initiatives under PM's Science Technology Innovation Advisory Council (PMSTIAC). Aim: Seed, nurture and scale scientific and industrial R&D in Quantum Technology. QpiAI-Indus: India's first 25-qubit superconducting quantum computer launched by QpiAI startup (selected under NQM); also developed India's first quantum computing chip QPU (Quantum Processing Unit). Quantum Key Distribution (QKD): Demonstrated over existing optical fibre by I-Hub Quantum Technology Foundation under NM-ICPS (National Mission on Interdisciplinary Cyber-Physical Systems). QKD mechanism: Uses quantum mechanics principles to ensure secure encryption key distribution - any eavesdropping attempt disturbs the quantum state of transmitted photons, making interception detectable. QSIP (Quantum Random Number Generator System in Package): Quantum security chip that generates certified randomness using quantum processes rather than mathematical algorithms - critical because cryptographic security depends on unpredictable random numbers that classical computers cannot truly generate. Quantum information Scrambling: Refers to rapid spread of information across quantum system through entanglement, making it inaccessible to local measurements; a 'Quantum Echoes' algorithm demonstrated information retention through entanglement - significant for understanding quantum advantage. UPSC: Quantum technology policy, NQM, cryptography, technology indigenisation."],
      ["Brain-Computer Interface, Neuralink Blindsight and neural technology frontier", "Brain-Computer Interface (BCI): Technology that creates direct communication pathway between brain neural activity and external devices or computers. Stanford's privacy-focused BCI: Requires no permanent implant; addresses ethical concerns about continuous neural data collection from implanted devices. Neuralink's Blindsight: Microelectrode array implanted in visual cortex (region of cerebral cortex processing visual information from retinas); aims to restore vision by bypassing damaged retinas entirely; stimulates neurons in visual cortex directly to create perception of light and shapes; first human implant targeted for 2025. Visual cortex: Located in occipital lobe; receives and processes visual data relayed from retinas via optic nerves; Blindsight's approach of directly stimulating this region could help individuals whose optic nerve or retina is damaged but whose visual cortex is intact. Ethical concerns about BCIs: Mental privacy (who owns brain data?), cognitive liberty (right not to be neurologically monitored), security (BCIs could theoretically be hacked), enhancement vs treatment distinction. Accelerometer technology: Google's Android Earthquake Alert system detected 18,000+ earthquakes using phone accelerometers; accelerometers measure acceleration using Newton's second law (Force = mass x acceleration); used in phones for screen orientation, fitness tracking, navigation."],
      ["Jevons Paradox, Solow Paradox and AI's economic implications", "Jevons Paradox: William Stanley Jevons (1865) observed that improved coal engine efficiency led to MORE coal consumption, not less - cheaper energy per unit led to increased adoption and use. Modern relevance: More fuel-efficient cars led to more driving; more energy-efficient AI chips led to more AI training runs; energy-efficient data centres led to larger data centres. Applied to AI: Efficiency improvements in AI models (smaller models doing more per watt) may increase total AI energy consumption because they enable more applications and wider deployment. Solow Paradox: Robert Solow (1987) noted 'you can see the computer age everywhere except in the productivity statistics' - widespread technology adoption did not produce expected productivity gains in 1980s. Modern AI analogy: Despite massive AI investment and deployment, measured productivity growth in economies has not accelerated proportionally. Resolution theories: Technology productivity gains take time to propagate as businesses learn to reorganise around new tools; national GDP statistics may not capture digital economic gains. Bharat Gen: India's first indigenous AI multimodal LLM for Indian languages; covers all 22 scheduled languages; aims for Bharat-centric data; developed under NM-ICPS through IIT Bombay's TIH Foundation. IndiaAI Mission (2024): 7-pillar framework implemented by IndiaAI IBD under Digital India Corporation. UPSC: Technology economics, AI policy, digital governance."],
      ["3D Printing, Digital Twin and emerging manufacturing technologies", "3D Printing (Additive Manufacturing): Creates three-dimensional objects from digital files by layering materials; contrast with subtractive manufacturing (cutting from solid block). Japan's station: Entire train station 3D printed in 6 hours - demonstrates infrastructure-scale application addressing Japan's shrinking workforce. Advantages: Immediate concept realisation (design to object quickly), reduced workforce dependency, on-demand customisation, reduced material waste compared to subtractive methods. Applications: Aerospace components, medical implants, construction, food manufacturing. Digital Twin: A digital representation of a physical object, person or process in its digital environment; uses real-time sensor data to simulate behaviour and monitor operations; enables organisations to test scenarios and make better decisions before physical changes; applications in factory monitoring, urban planning, aircraft maintenance. SSI Mantra: India's indigenous surgical robotic system performed world's first cardiac telesurgery across 10,000 km; key features include 3D high-definition visualisation and predictive analytics. Relevance of SSI Mantra to India: Telesurgery enables specialist procedures in remote areas without patient transport; indigenous development reduces dependence on expensive imported surgical robots. ADAS (Advanced Driver Assistance Systems): Suite of sensors (Lidar, Radar, cameras) enabling pedestrian detection, lane departure warning, adaptive cruise control, emergency braking - precursor to autonomous vehicles. UPSC: Technology governance, manufacturing innovation, digital transformation."],
      ["Stablecoins, CBDC and India's digital finance architecture", "Stablecoins: Type of cryptocurrency whose value is pegged to another asset - most commonly the US dollar (e.g., Tether/USDT), gold, or a basket of currencies. Types: Fiat-collateralised (backed by real currency reserves), crypto-collateralised (backed by other cryptocurrencies), algorithmic (maintain peg through supply adjustments without collateral). Risks: Mass withdrawal (bank run equivalent), failure of elasticity (cannot quickly expand supply in high demand), money laundering risk, systemic risk to payment systems. India's cryptocurrency regulation: Currently unregulated for ownership but taxed - Finance Act 2022 imposed 30% tax on capital gains from Virtual Digital Assets (VDAs); 1% TDS on transfers; defined under Income Tax Act as any cryptographic information or code. VDAs brought under Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA) in 2023 - FIU registration required. CBDC (Central Bank Digital Currency): Digital form of fiat currency issued by central bank; not a cryptocurrency (it is government liability like cash). RBI's e-rupee pilots: Retail CBDC available via digital wallets and smartphone apps; Wholesale CBDC for interbank payments and securities transactions. Stablecoins vs CBDC: Stablecoins are private liabilities; CBDCs are government liabilities with legal tender status; India developing CBDC as regulated alternative to private stablecoins. Bluetooth Mesh Networking (Bitchat): Uses Bluetooth Low Energy Mesh for device-to-device communication without internet infrastructure; messages stored on devices not central server - privacy-preserving communication model. UPSC: Digital economy, fintech regulation, RBI's digital currency policy."]
    ],
    qs: [
      { q: "India's QpiAI demonstrating a 25-qubit superconducting quantum computer under the National Quantum Mission represents early-stage progress. The Quantum Key Distribution demonstration over existing optical fibre is considered more immediately practically significant because:", opts: ["QKD is easier to develop than a full quantum computer and represents a lower-ambition milestone that India can achieve while the more difficult quantum computing programme continues in parallel, a policy design consideration the Survey raises when distinguishing between temporary PLI-type interventions calibrated to specific learning curve dynamics and permanent protective tariffs that generate rent-seeking without building productive capability","QKD addresses a concrete near-term national security need - protecting sensitive communications from future quantum computers that could break current encryption; demonstrating QKD over existing fibre infrastructure means India can deploy quantum-secure communication across its existing telecom network without rebuilding infrastructure, providing defensive security benefit long before quantum computing advantage is commercially available","QKD using existing optical fibre suggests India's quantum technology is ready for commercial licensing to telecom companies, generating revenue that can fund the more expensive quantum computing programme under NQM, a structural tension the Survey identifies when explaining why standard macroeconomic instruments cannot resolve supply-side vulnerabilities that arise from geographic concentration of production capacity in critical input sectors","Quantum computers require cooling to near absolute zero (-273°C) making them impractical for field deployment, while QKD devices operate at room temperature and can immediately replace conventional network encryption hardware in Indian defence and banking networks, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "Neuralink's Blindsight chip aims to restore vision for people with damaged retinas by stimulating the visual cortex directly. Which feature of the visual system's anatomy makes this bypass approach theoretically feasible for some forms of blindness but not all?", opts: ["The visual cortex can process electrical stimulation signals from electrodes identically to how it processes optical signals from the retina because the brain cannot distinguish between stimuli from different sources once they arrive as action potentials, reflecting the broader challenge of institutional capacity that runs through multiple sections of the Survey's analysis of India's development constraints and growth enablers","This approach works specifically for conditions where the visual cortex itself is intact but the upstream pathway (retina, optic nerve) is damaged - Blindsight bypasses the damaged components by delivering signals directly to the cortical processing stage; it cannot help people with damaged visual cortex such as those who have suffered occipital lobe strokes, because no peripheral bypass can substitute for the absent processing tissue","The visual cortex has neuroplasticity that allows it to be retrained to interpret electrical patterns as visual images through machine learning algorithms, meaning Blindsight works for any type of blindness regardless of the anatomical location of damage by teaching new signal interpretation","Blindsight works because retinal photoreceptors can be regenerated from stem cells injected into the cortex through the electrode array, effectively growing a new retina inside the brain rather than stimulating existing cortical tissue, which reflects the Survey's broader argument about balancing short-term efficiency costs against long-term strategic resilience in sectors where supply chain concentration creates asymmetric vulnerability"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Jevons Paradox applied to AI energy consumption suggests that more energy-efficient AI models may paradoxically increase total AI energy use. What policy implication does this create for India's AI governance approach?", opts: ["India should discourage AI efficiency research and instead mandate that all AI systems use legacy hardware, keeping per-computation energy costs high enough to limit deployment and thus constrain total energy consumption, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","If efficiency improvements drive total consumption growth rather than reducing it, energy governance cannot rely solely on improving AI efficiency standards - India's AI policy may need to address total computational resource use (as the EU's AI Act attempts) rather than just per-operation efficiency, while ensuring this does not disadvantage India's emerging AI sector relative to US and Chinese competitors who face fewer restrictions","The Jevons Paradox suggests that efficiency-focused AI governance will be insufficient on its own, and India should supplement efficiency standards with broader digital economy frameworks that monitor aggregate computational consumption trends and adjust policy if total AI energy use grows faster than renewable energy supply, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into","AI energy efficiency improvements should be patented internationally by India to capture royalty revenue, which could then fund renewable energy infrastructure expansion to supply the additional energy that Jevons Paradox predicts AI efficiency will generate demand for, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "India's RBI is piloting both retail and wholesale Central Bank Digital Currency (e-rupee) while private stablecoins remain unregulated but taxed. What structural difference between CBDCs and stablecoins is most significant for financial stability regulation?", opts: ["Stablecoins use blockchain technology which RBI cannot monitor, while CBDCs use a central ledger that RBI fully controls - the technological difference is the primary regulatory concern rather than the ownership structure, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale needed for technological learning while maintaining the competitive pressure needed for efficiency","A CBDC is a direct liability of the Reserve Bank of India backed by the government's sovereign capacity - like physical rupee notes, its value is guaranteed by state authority; a stablecoin is a private company's liability whose peg to the dollar depends on the company maintaining sufficient reserves, and if users lose confidence and simultaneously redeem their holdings (a digital bank run), the stablecoin can collapse entirely, potentially destabilising payment systems as happened with TerraUSD in 2022","CBDCs require internet connectivity while stablecoins can be used offline, making stablecoins more appropriate for rural financial inclusion in India's unbanked population where digital infrastructure is unreliable, a comparative development insight the Survey draws from East Asian economies that successfully combined domestic market protection with export orientation, creating the scale needed for technological learning while maintaining the competitive pressure needed for efficiency","The difference between CBDCs and stablecoins is primarily one of regulatory oversight depth rather than fundamental financial stability risk, since well-designed stablecoins with full reserve backing and real-time audit capabilities can achieve similar safety profiles to CBDCs while preserving the privacy and programmability features that make private digital currencies attractive for specific use cases, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size"], ans: 1 },
      { q: "The Solow Paradox (technology adoption not reflected in productivity statistics) is being re-examined in the context of AI. Which explanation for this phenomenon, if correct, would be most reassuring for India's long-term growth outlook from AI investment?", opts: ["If Solow Paradox occurs because technology adoption actually reduces productivity by distracting workers from their primary tasks, India's strategy of limiting AI adoption to narrow specific use cases rather than general deployment would be the correct policy response, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","If the Solow Paradox reflects a genuine measurement problem where GDP statistics systematically undercount digital economic gains rather than a real absence of productivity growth, then India's AI investments may be generating real welfare improvements and competitive advantages that appear only weakly in national accounts - the economic benefits are real but diffuse across many sectors in ways that show up in quality-of-life measures before they appear in aggregate productivity statistics","The Solow Paradox suggests that technology investments take much longer to show up in productivity statistics than economists initially forecast, meaning India should interpret the absence of near-term productivity gains from AI investments as consistent with significant long-term impact rather than as evidence of policy failure, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool","If the Paradox occurs because technology productivity gains require complete business process reorganisation that takes decades, India should pause all AI deployment until a comprehensive national reorganisation plan is designed and mandated for all sectors simultaneously, a governance reform imperative the Survey identifies as prerequisite for translating India's demographic and market size advantages into sustained productivity growth rather than simply into a larger low-wage labour pool"], ans: 1 }
    ]
  }

